  "notes": "South has 12 top tricks (3 spades, the ♥A, the ♦A, and 7 club tricks). North's ♠10 is the only hope for a thirteenth trick.\n\nAfter winning the opening lead and drawing trump, South must decide on how to play spades. South could cash the ♠A, ♠K, and ♠Q hoping that the ♠J drops, making the ♠10 high. Alternatively, South could play West for the ♠J, finessing the ♠10.\n\nFinessing the ♠10 is in fact the percentage play. The finesse is necessary when West has the ♠J and length in the suit. Playing for the drop is necessary when East has the ♠J and shortness in the suit. Since the ♠J is more likely to be with length than with shortness, South should finesse.\n\nA top honour in spades should be cashed first in case East has a singleton ♠J.",
